{"hash":"7b280340caccd272","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":92.645,"said":"This stepped tank is Kotitirtha. Its water is used in Mahakal’s worship, and during Shiv Navratri each day’s rituals begin with abhishek right here. Look into it for a moment, because it has seen the hardest chapter of this temple’s life. Under the Paramara kings of Malwa, from around the tenth century, the temple was at its most splendid. Then, nearly eight hundred years ago, in 1234 and 1235, the Delhi sultan Iltutmish raided Ujjain and destroyed the temple. What became of the Jyotirlinga? The accounts differ. A Persian chronicler of that age wrote that images were carried away to Delhi; the people of Ujjain have long held that the Jyotirlinga was thrown into this very kund. Either way, the great temple was gone. And yet, for some five hundred years, pilgrims kept coming. Then, in the 1730s, after the Marathas took Malwa, Ranoji Rao Shinde entrusted the administration to his diwan, Ramchandra Baba Shenvi, and he rebuilt the temple whose form you see today. Carry this water’s story with you, as Ujjain has for eight hundred years: the temple fell, and the city rebuilt it around the same Lord. Jai Shri Mahakal.\n\nDid you know? The temple’s ash for the Bhasma Aarti, and its daily flag-hoisting, are traditionally the service of the Shri Panchayati Mahanirvani Akhada.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1127},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1143,"end":1283}],"words":[[0.098,0.252,0,4],[0.362,0.385,5,12],[0.759,0.325,13,17],[1.096,0.144,18,20],[1.252,0.841,21,31],[2.993,0.204,33,36],[3.209,0.397,37,42],[3.618,0.168,43,45],[3.798,0.312,46,50],[4.123,0.108,51,53],[4.243,0.589,54,63],[4.844,0.505,64,71],[5.565,0.216,73,76],[5.793,0.373,77,83],[6.178,0.276,84,88],[6.466,0.661,89,97],[7.139,0.288,98,102],[7.44,0.325,103,108],[7.776,0.445,109,116],[8.233,0.349,117,122],[8.594,0.288,123,127],[8.894,0.505,128,136],[9.411,0.3,137,142],[9.724,0.288,143,147],[10.889,0.252,149,153